      "text": "Let \\( h \\) be the number of hours we are looking for. Clock equivalent to h hours means that \\( h \\) is \"clock equivalent\" to \\( h^2 \\) hours. This relationship can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ h \\equiv h^2 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\nRewriting the congruence, we get:\n\n\\[ h^2 - h \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\nFactoring out \\( h \\), we obtain:\n\n\\[ h(h - 1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\nThis means that \\( h \\) or \\( h - 1 \\) must be divisible by 12. Therefore, \\( h \\) must be one of the following values:\n\\[ h \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\nor\n\\[ h \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\nAdditionally, \\( h \\) must be greater than 4. Let's check the smallest values for \\( h \\) that satisfy these conditions:\n1. If \\( h = 12 \\), then \\( h(h - 1) = 12(12 - 1) = 12 \\times 11 = 132 \\), and \\( 132 \\div 12 = 11 \\), so \\( 132 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n2. If \\( h = 1 \\), then \\( h(h - 1) = 1(1 - 1) = 1 \\times 0 = 0 \\), and \\( 0 \\div 12 = 0 \\), so \\( 0 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nThe least whole number of hours that is greater than 4 hours and is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours is \\( h = 1 \\).\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
