      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest whole number \\( n > 4 \\) such that \\( n \\) is \"clock equivalent\" to \\( n^2 \\) on a 12-hour clock. This means that \\( n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nFirst, let's simplify the congruence equation:\n\\[ n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\]\nRearranging, we get:\n\\[ n^2 - n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\\[ n(n-1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\nThis means that \\( n(n-1) \\) must be divisible by 12. Since \\( n \\) and \\( n-1 \\) are consecutive integers, one of them must be divisible by 3 and one of them must be divisible by 4. Additionally, since \\( n \\) and \\( n-1 \\) are relatively prime, one of them must be divisible by 3 and one of them must be divisible by 4.\n\nWe will check the values of \\( n \\) starting from 5 to find the smallest value that satisfies this condition.\n\n1. For \\( n = 5 \\):\n\\[ 5(5-1) = 5 \\times 4 = 20 \\]\nSince \\( 20 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 5 \\) is not a solution.\n\n2. For \\( n = 6 \\):\n\\[ 6(6-1) = 6 \\times 5 = 30 \\]\nSince \\( 30 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 6 \\) is not a solution.\n\n3. For \\( n = 7 \\):\n\\[ 7(7-1) = 7 \\times 6 = 42 \\]\nSince \\( 42 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 7 \\) is not a solution.\n\n4. For \\( n = 8 \\):\n\\[ 8(8-1) = 8 \\times 7 = 56 \\]\nSince \\( 56 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 8 \\) is not a solution.\n\n5. For \\( n = 9 \\):\n\\[ 9(9-1) = 9 \\times 8 = 72 \\]\nSince \\( 72 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 9 \\) is a solution because \\( 9 \\times 9 = 81 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{12} \\), which does not satisfy the condition. However, \\( 9 \\times 9 = 81 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{12} \\), which actually means \\( 9 \\times 9 = 81 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{12} \\), so we need to check further.\n\n6. For \\( n = 10 \\):\n\\[ 10(10-1) = 10 \\times 9 = 90 \\]\nSince \\( 90 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 10 \\) is not a solution.\n\n7. For \\( n = 11 \\):\n\\[ 11(11-1) = 11 \\times 10 = 110 \\]\nSince \\( 110 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 11 \\) is not a solution.\n\n8. For \\( n = 12 \\):\n\\[ 12(12-1) = 12 \\times 11 = 132 \\]\nSince \\( 132 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 12 \\) is a solution because \\( 12 \\times 12 = 144 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nWe need to check if 12 is the smallest solution:\n- For \\( n = 13 \\):\n\\[ 13(13-1) = 13 \\times 12 = 156 \\]\nSince \\( 156 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{12} \\), \\( n = 13 \\) is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the smallest whole number of hours that is greater than 4 hours and is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
