      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the least whole number of hours greater than 4 such that the elapsed time of four hours is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours. This means we need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 4 \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\), where \\( n \\) is a positive integer greater than 4. Let's start by checking the squares of the integers greater than 4:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&6^2 = 36 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}, \\\\\n&7^2 = 49 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12}, \\\\\n&8^2 = 64 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{12}, \\\\\n&9^2 = 81 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{12}, \\\\\n&10^2 = 100 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{12}, \\\\\n&11^2 = 121 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFrom the above calculations, we see that the smallest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 4 \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\) is \\( n = 8 \\) because \\( 8^2 = 64 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{12} \\). Therefore, the least whole number of hours that is greater than 4 hours and is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]",
